1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,509
Java records represent small data carriers such as Context, Decision, and Result.

2
00:00:05,659 --> 00:00:10,011
An interface separates a capability from its implementation: Agent.Model can be backed

3
00:00:10,011 --> 00:00:14,363
by a queue for tests or an HTTP adapter for live inference.

4
00:00:14,513 --> 00:00:19,494
Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit and allows a fixed clock in tests.

5
00:00:19,644 --> 00:00:25,051
Exceptions represent rejected input, exhausted budgets, and dependency failures.

6
00:00:25,201 --> 00:00:27,934
Use immutable snapshots across boundaries.

7
00:00:28,084 --> 00:00:32,963
List.copyOf prevents a model adapter from changing the engine’s history collection, although

8
00:00:32,963 --> 00:00:36,622
it does not magically make arbitrary nested objects immutable.

9
00:00:36,772 --> 00:00:39,470
Map.copyOf rejects null keys and values.

10
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:44,594
Keep account identity in trusted application context rather than accepting it from

11
00:00:44,594 --> 00:00:45,424
generated text.

12
00:00:45,574 --> 00:00:50,470
Generic collections preserve types at compile time, while JSON entering from a

13
00:00:50,470 --> 00:00:52,510
network still needs runtime validation.

14
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:56,013
Agent.run receives Context with tenant red and user alice.

15
00:00:56,163 --> 00:00:59,498
SupportTools gets that same context for every tool call.

16
00:00:59,648 --> 00:01:04,277
A generated tenant field is rejected because lookupOrder accepts only orderId.

17
00:01:04,427 --> 00:01:08,245
This separation is more valuable than putting an instruction saying never read

18
00:01:08,245 --> 00:01:09,836
another tenant in the prompt.

19
00:01:09,986 --> 00:01:13,158
Find every mutable collection in Agent and ApprovalLedger.

20
00:01:13,308 --> 00:01:16,121
Add a test that a model cannot add a message to its

21
00:01:16,121 --> 00:01:16,590
input history.

22
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:19,907
Explain why synchronized is used in the in-memory ledger.

23
00:01:20,057 --> 00:01:23,829
The engine owns its ArrayList and gives the model an immutable snapshot.

24
00:01:23,979 --> 00:01:29,413
The ledger synchronizes a compound check-and-record operation so concurrent approvals produce one

25
00:01:29,413 --> 00:01:29,866
receipt.

26
00:01:30,016 --> 00:01:35,922
This protects one JVM only; durable cross-instance coordination requires a database transaction

27
00:01:35,922 --> 00:01:37,399
or equivalent authority.

28
00:01:37,549 --> 00:01:38,220
Question.

29
00:01:38,370 --> 00:01:39,591
Why inject Clock?

30
00:01:39,741 --> 00:01:40,318
Answer.

31
00:01:40,468 --> 00:01:45,097
Expiry and deadlines become deterministic test inputs instead of tests that sleep.

32
00:01:45,247 --> 00:01:45,919
Question.

33
00:01:46,069 --> 00:01:48,641
Does a record make all contained data immutable?

34
00:01:48,791 --> 00:01:49,367
Answer.

35
00:01:49,517 --> 00:01:50,001
No.

36
00:01:50,151 --> 00:01:54,182
Its references are final, but a mutable collection held by a record

37
00:01:54,182 --> 00:01:56,870
can still change unless copied or wrapped appropriately.
